  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev09-04",
    "question": "<p>(a) Express <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{1}{r(r+2)}\"></span> in partial fractions.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>(b) Hence prove, by the method of differences, that</p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} \\frac{1}{r(r+2)}=\\frac{n(an+b)}{4(n+1)(n+2)}\"></div><p>where a and b are constants to be found.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(6)</span></p><p>(c) Hence show that</p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=n+1}^{2n} \\frac{1}{r(r+2)}=\\frac{n(4n+5)}{4(n+1)(n+2)(2n+1)}\"></div><p><span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>Substituting <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r=0\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r=-2\"></span> in the cleared polynomial identity isolates the two coefficients immediately. Although these are poles of the original fractions, the cleared identity is a polynomial equality. In the tail sum the same two-step cancellation occurs at new endpoints.</p>",
      "<p>(a) Clearing denominators gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1=A(r+2)+Br\"></span>, so <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A=1/2,\\ B=-1/2\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) Sum <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\tfrac12(1/r-1/(r+2))\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The first terms are <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(1-1/3)+(1/2-1/4)+(1/3-1/5)\"></span> inside the half factor. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>All but the first two positive and last two negative terms cancel. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The sum is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\tfrac12(3/2-1/(n+1)-1/(n+2))\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Combining fractions gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n(3n+5)/[4(n+1)(n+2)]\"></span>, hence <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a=3,b=5\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(c) From <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n+1\"></span> to <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2n\"></span>, retain <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\tfrac12[1/(n+1)+1/(n+2)-1/(2n+1)-1/(2n+2)]\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Putting over a common denominator gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n(4n+5)/[4(n+1)(n+2)(2n+1)]\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>For example, at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=2\"></span> the sum in (b) is <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1/3+1/8=11/24\"></span>. Substituting <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=2\"></span> in <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n(3n+5)/[4(n+1)(n+2)]\"></span> also gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"22/48=11/24\"></span>, checking both end terms of the cancellation.</p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 11 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev09-04-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2012 · Q6, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev09-05",
    "question": "<p>(a) Express <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\frac{2}{(2 r+1)(2 r+3)}\"></span> in partial fractions.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p><p>(b) Using your answer to (a), find, in terms of n,</p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} \\frac{3}{(2 r+1)(2 r+3)}\"></div><p>Give your answer as a single fraction in its simplest form.</p><p><span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>Consecutive indices give denominators <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2r+3\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2(r+1)+1\"></span>, which are identical. The factor <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3/2\"></span> changes the numerator <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2\"></span> from the difference into the required <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3\"></span>. At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=1\"></span> the final expression gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1/5\"></span>, agreeing with the first term.</p>",
      "<p>(a) With a common denominator <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1/(2r+1)-1/(2r+3)\"></span> has numerator 2. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) Multiply by <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"3/2\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The differences cancel successively, giving <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\tfrac32(1/3-1/(2n+3))\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Simplifying gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n/(2n+3)\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 5 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev09-05-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for 2013 · Q1, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  }
